NAME
    memcard - manage memory cards, paks and a console's save memory

SYNOPSIS
    memcard ls [<card>]
    memcard cp <card>:<save> <card>
    memcard mv <card>:<save> <card>
    memcard rm <card>:<save>
    memcard import <card> <from>...
    memcard new <card>
    memcard dup <card> <new card>
    memcard rename <card> <new card>

DESCRIPTION
    The card manager of every console that keeps saves on a medium of its
    own: the PlayStation's memory cards, and as their cores declare them
    the N64's Controller Paks and the Sega CD's backup RAM. A <card> is a
    file in the sandbox, named as 'ls' and 'cp' name it:

        Saves/PlayStation/Cards/Card 1.mcr     a shared or named card
        Saves/PlayStation/Amber Saga.mcr       a game's own card
        Saves/Sega CD/Console/Backup RAM 1.brm the console's own memory

    Its medium is known by its extension, from the console's system pack.
    A <save> is the save's name on the card, or its number in 'memcard ls'.

    'ls' with no card lists every card under Saves by console, with its
    medium, its free space and whether a game holds it; with a card, the
    saves on it, each with its size, its name and its title. 'cp' copies
    a save onto another card of the same medium; 'mv' copies it, reads it
    back from the second card, and only then deletes it from the first.
    'rm' deletes a save as the console's own manager does: the PlayStation
    card's directory marks it deleted and keeps its blocks.

    'import' copies every save of the <from> cards onto <card>, ALL OR
    NONE. It is refused whole when the saves need more than the card's
    free space (15 blocks on a PlayStation card), with each save and the
    blocks it needs listed, or when two of the cards hold different saves
    of one name; a save already on <card> is left as it is. The <from>
    cards are read and never written, so a game's own card stays as it was
    and Per game, chosen again in Graphics Settings, finds it unchanged.

    'new' makes a card under a name, formatted where the medium's module
    can write its format; otherwise the name is the card, and the game
    formats it when it first saves, as a new card. 'dup' copies a whole
    card to a new name and 'rename' renames one; neither needs to read
    the medium's format, so they work for every medium.

THE LOCK
    A running game holds a lock on each card it was given, <card>.lock
    beside the file, for as long as it runs. Every write refuses a card
    that is held, and says by whom: "in use by Amber Saga" for a game of
    this program, "in use by another program" otherwise. Close the game,
    then change its cards; the machine would otherwise write over them.

FORMATS
    A medium's saves are read by its format module, pure functions over
    the card's bytes: the PlayStation's (psx-spx, "Memory Card Data
    Format"). A medium with no module yet (the Sega CD's, the N64's) lists
    its size, and offers only new, dup and rename.

EXAMPLES
    memcard ls
    cd Saves/PlayStation
    memcard new Cards/Blue.mcr
    memcard ls "Amber Saga.mcr"
    memcard cp "Amber Saga.mcr:1" Cards/Blue.mcr
    memcard import Cards/Blue.mcr "Amber Saga.mcr" "Basalt Run.mcr"

